{"valid":true,"reason":"The claim holds. `compactHeadViewLocked` takes the watermark from `iso.lastAppendID()`, which returns the highest append ID handed out so far, including IDs held by appenders that have not committed yet. Each block writer takes an isolation snapshot (`iso.State`) that lists still-open appends as incomplete, so their samples are left out of the block.

The failure: an appender is opened before the watermark is read, then commits an in-order sample with ts <= maxt after the block is written. That sample's append ID is <= the watermark, so `hasAppendIDAbove` returns false. `truncateSelectedSeries` has no other check that catches it (the maxTime check passes because ts <= maxt), so the series is evicted and a WAL tombstone is written. The sample is in neither the block nor the head, and is also dropped on WAL replay after restart. This breaks the documented guarantee that series receiving new samples after compaction starts are skipped.

For `truncateStaleSeries`, `isStaleSeries` usually catches the late sample, unless that sample is itself a stale marker. The window is a concurrency race, so I rate it medium. Caveat: `head_append.go` isn't in this checkout. That append IDs are assigned when the appender is created (not at commit) comes from upstream Prometheus, not from code I could read here.","severity":"medium"}
